WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.210
In this last episode, I'll show how it's
built.

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.582
There are six projects, every dependency
points inward,

3
00:00:08.582 --> 00:00:12.278
and the core knows nothing about OpenCV,
ONNX Runtime or WPF,

4
00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:15.610
so most of the tests run without a GPU or
a video file.

5
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.639
I used to keep that rule by review, but

6
00:00:25.639 --> 00:00:28.273
now a test checks it for the core
projects,

7
00:00:28.273 --> 00:00:30.050
and CI runs it on every push.

8
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.130
Three models, but the app sees one
interface.

9
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:47.052
When I replaced OpenCV's video writer with
FFmpeg,

10
00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:50.191
I added one adapter and changed one
registration,

11
00:00:50.191 --> 00:00:54.930
and the new writer falls back to the old
one whenever it can't do the job.

12
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.810
Here's an export, with FFmpeg encoding.

13
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.610
The UI thread never decodes or blurs,
while playback,

14
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.802
analysis and export each run their own
tasks,

15
00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:14.207
joined by bounded queues with their own
decoder,

16
00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:18.250
and playback never holds more than nine
frames by design.

17
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.890
Right now, only the face detection
actually runs on the GPU.

18
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.923
The GPU only runs where it measured a win,
like detection at five times the CPU,

19
00:01:35.923 --> 00:01:39.538
while the blur on the integrated GPU was
actually slower,

20
00:01:39.538 --> 00:01:43.850
so the next step is keeping frames on the
GPU from decode to export.

21
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.192
Two of my measurements lied. In the first,

22
00:01:53.192 --> 00:01:57.150
the same code and clip showed twenty-six
percent of frames over budget,

23
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.610
then under one percent, because the PC was
busy and a stray process held a core.

24
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.403
The second was an average with a cold
start,

25
00:02:09.403 --> 00:02:12.595
where one slow first call made a fast
lookup look slow,

26
00:02:12.595 --> 00:02:15.090
so now I report medians and recent
windows.

27
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:26.650
These are the patterns, each one next to
the class that needed it.

28
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.810
And these are the numbers I held each
choice to.

29
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.533
The cost is more projects and some
indirection,

30
00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:47.330
and the integration tests only run on my
machine.

31
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:55.170
Next comes the whole pipeline on the GPU.
Thanks for following along.

